# Drive the arm to a joint-space pose and hold it there. This is the only way
# to ask arm_control for motion -- callers state a pose, never a velocity.
#
# Goal
# ----
# name/position are parallel arrays, matching the convention of
# JointVelocityCommand: order does not matter, but every one of the five
# known joints (base_yaw, shoulder_pitch, elbow_pitch, head_pan, head_tilt)
# must appear exactly once. There is no partial-pose goal -- a caller that
# only cares about the head still states where the base should stay, so a
# new goal can never leave a joint drifting on an old target.
string[] name
float64[] position
---
# Result
# ------
# state is the terminal value of the same enum ~/status and the feedback
# below use: MOVING, SETTLED, STOPPED, FAULTED (see ArmStatus.msg). SETTLED
# is the only success; STOPPED means a newer goal or a cancel preempted this
# one before it settled; FAULTED means the joint-state input went stale or
# invalid before this goal settled. MOVING never appears here -- it is not a
# terminal state.
string state
string[] name
float64[] position
---
# Feedback
# --------
# Published on every control tick this goal is active for. state is always
# MOVING here -- ~/status is where SETTLED/STOPPED/FAULTED show up, since
# none of them describe an in-progress goal. error is position - target, in
# the same order as name.
string state
string[] name
float64[] position
float64[] velocity
float64[] error
